1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 { The Italian game is one of the oldest openings in chess. It was developed in the 16th and 17th centuries by famous Italian masters Damiano, Polerio, and Greco. It has always been frequently seen in beginners' and amateurs' games but its popularity among strong players gradually ceased in favor of the Ruy Lopez, which was considered a better try for an advantage until very recently. However, things have changed dramatically in recent years. Due to the futility of their attempts against the Berlin Defence, top players have started seeking ways to pose Black problems in the long-forgotten Italian. As a result of the trend set by Carlsen, Anand, Caruana, Karjakin, Vachier- Lagrave, Giri and other strong players it has suddenly become more popular than the Ruy Lopez and its theory has grown enormously. Unlike just a few years ago, Black is nowadays required to know the nuances of different lines and move orders to obtain a good position out of the opening. } Bc5 4. c3 { In this chapter we will focus on the most straightforward 5. d4. In the next one we will cover 5. d3 while 4. O-O will be seen in later chapters. -KEY- } Nf6 5. d4 { Going for the d4 break immediately is the old way of playing the Italian. Nowadays it is seen mostly at lower levels, because it has long been known not to give White any realistic hopes for an advantage. } exd4 6. e5 { A modern treatment of the 5. d4 line. } d5 7. Be2 { This seemingly innocuous move gains in popularity lately. White players like to experiment with different things, even if it objectively doesn't give any advantage it may have a surprise value. } d3 $1 { Played in order to obtain a favourable structure. } 8. Bxd3 $2 Ng4 9. O-O Ncxe5 $17 { White is simply a pawn down. } 
